From 3c27888078b70ec48e2f370fe64c6bc69139763a Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: allcodernet <67543055+allcodernet@users.noreply.github.com>
Date: Fri, 25 Sep 2026 13:51:24 +0200
Subject: [PATCH 05/45] decide-trie: tokenisation boundary rule, sibling check,
 quote-split fallback

Add decide-trie.{h,cpp}: build_trie tokenises stem_text(f) + each
option's encoded text (add_special=false, parse_special=false), takes
the longest common token prefix as the stem, builds a trie from the
remaining per-option paths, and enumerates branch nodes (>= 2
children) in pre-order. At every branch node, a sibling-prefix check
compares the rendered piece of each child token pairwise; if any pair
is a text prefix of the other, the field is ambiguous under greedy
decoding.

For choice fields, a conflict falls back to a quote-anchored split:
stem = tokenize("  \"name\": \"") and each option's path is tokenized
on its own starting after the opening quote, which keeps keys that
merge with the quote glyph (e.g. sales on SentencePiece-style
tokenizers) usable at the cost of an unnatural token boundary
(field_trie::quote_split, reported by the engine later). Score and
bool fields never use the fallback, since their encoded values (an
integer index or true/false) don't share the quote-anchoring
rationale. If the conflict persists after the fallback, build_trie
throws invalid_schema naming both options.

branch_tokens(t, node) walks from a trie node up to the root and
returns stem + the edge tokens in root-to-node order, for the engine to
fill branch sequences. tokenize_plain/tokenize_template wrap
common_tokenize with the add_special/parse_special flags the rest of
the engine needs.

Tested against both Qwen2.5-0.5B-Instruct (BPE/gpt2) and Gemma 4
vocab_only GGUFs: the triage schema's queue/urgency/angry fields all
take the joint tokenisation path on both families (quote_split never
needed for the shipped fixtures); a "sale"/"sales" choice field
conflicts and is rejected on both after the fallback is attempted
(confirms the fallback path executes and still correctly detects a
genuine ambiguity); a 255-option choice field builds on both.
